{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8af3671909c06727","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9004\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9004\n10\t\n11\t## Review Summary\n12\t\n13\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n14\tRecommendation: accept\n15\t\n16\t## Criterion Details\n17\t\n18\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n19\t\n20\tAll three HLRs (HLR-1 P1, HLR-2 P1, HLR-3 P2) map to RHAISTRAT-9004-E001, and the traceability matrix in the decomposition summary is present and complete. Priority inheritance: E001 is a `docs-authoring` epic, so priority derives from the strategy's Jira priority — `Major` → P1, correctly applied and explicitly documented in the summary. The P2 troubleshooting HLR (HLR-3) is bundled with P1 work, but the strategy is triaged `docs-only`, so the single-epic bundle is correct by design and priority collapse is not flagged per the triage exception. No orphaned requirements.\n21\t\n22\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n23\t\n24\tSingle-epic decomposition with `dependencies: []` in frontmatter and a single-node, edge-free DAG diagram — they match. No circular dependencies, no invalid edges, no konflux/license-validation pairings to check. Critical path length 1 is appropriate for a triaged docs-only strategy. No data/artifact dependencies exist that are missing from the DAG (no upstream producers — source material already exists per the strategy).\n25\t\n26\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n27\t\n28\t`triage: docs-only` with `epic_count: 1` — consistent; the triage consistency check passes. The single epic spans one component (Documentation) and one team (RHOAI Serving). KServe is correctly treated as a passive/reference component (accuracy-validation AC) rather than a second epic. Authoring a single guide with five sections is well within a two-week window. No scope duplication (only one epic) and no work-product type mixing (the epic is purely content authoring; the technical review is an AC, not bundled implementation work).\n29\t\n30\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n31\t\n32\tE001 is typed `Implementation` with `implementation_type: docs-authoring` — correct for a strategy whose entire deliverable is published guidance. The epic produces a concrete artifact (the published disconnected-install guide). `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` are null and there are no Investigation dependencies, so gating metadata is not applicable. `investigation_signals: null` is correct for an Implementation epic.\n33\t\n34\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n35\t\n36\tE001 uses the 9-signal Implementation rubric (`ai_signals`), and the matching ai-signals rationale file exists with rationales that align with the frontmatter values. Spot-checks: `open_questions: 1` is correct (strategy explicitly states none); `human_process_gates: -1` is correct (mandatory technical review sign-off); `existing_foundation: 1` and `pattern_precedent: 1` are justified (serverless-mode disconnected docs and field enablement source material exist); `adapter_pattern: 0` (N/A) is correct. One minor observation on `architecture_claims: 1` — the rubric's +1 condition is \"strategy cites specific architecture context files,\" which the strategy text itself does not do; however, the decomposer's rationale is transparent that the claims were verified against `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` (which does substantiate raw-deployment/no-mesh and serverless-only operators), and the -1 condition (unsubstantiated claims) clearly does not apply. Value is defensible; noted for completeness, no score impact.\n37\t\n38\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n39\t\n40\tAll three HLRs have Given/When/Then acceptance criteria tied to measurable review conditions. The rule-mandated docs-authoring ACs are present: technical review and sign-off by the serving engineering team, plus a mandatory technical accuracy validation against the KServe architecture context (including the specific claims most at risk: no mesh dependency, serverless-only operators, serving runtime dependence). The out-of-scope constraint (no code/operator/config changes) is restated as an AC. No draft-ID sibling references in the epic body (HLR references are legitimate requirement IDs, not epic IDs).\n41\t\n42\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tEvery element of the strategy scope is covered: prerequisites, operator configuration (including which operators to skip), image-mirroring reference with per-release digest derivation, validation steps, and all three named troubleshooting failure modes (image pull errors, missing runtime, storage-initializer misconfiguration). Out-of-scope items (code changes, serverless disconnected support) are correctly excluded and restated. The docs-authoring requirement is satisfied by the epic itself. The strategy's context — no open questions, source material in field enablement docs, accuracy risk on digest lists — is carried forward into the epic description and ACs, and the summary's health warnings honestly record the strategy's missing sections (Impacted Teams, Dependencies, NFRs) rather than silently dropping them.\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8fa9fb03e35e581","agent_id":"ad0e239dc78b6743f","session_id":"1408b023-5547-4414-b80e-ccfb4877e639","uuid":"5672f496-a698-43ce-a38b-2feb5e14de5e","timestamp":"2026-10-21T21:24:48.172Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8af3671909c06727","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9004 decomposition"}
